<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="unit2-summary">
  <title>Unit 2 Summary</title>

   <subsection xml:id="unit2-summary-objectives">
    <title>Unit 2 Learning Objectives</title>

  <p>In Unit 2, we covered the following Learning Objectives:</p>
   <ul>
    <li><p>2.1 Program Design and Development 
    <ul>
      <li>2.1.A Develop a program using an iterative or incremental process</li>
      <li>2.1.B Design an app or program and its user interface based on user research and program requirements</li>
        <li>2.1.C Describe the purpose and behavior of a code segment or program by writing documentation</li>
        <li>2.1.D Determine when it is appropriate to reuse, cite, and share digital content</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>2.2 Boolean Expressions
    <ul>
        <li>2.2.A Identify the use of Boolean expressions to control an app’s behavior as you interact with the app</li>
        <li>2.2.B Develop code using relational operators, and determine the result of relational expressions</li>
        <li>2.2.C Develop code using logical operators, and determine the result of logical expressions</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>2.3 Selection
    <ul>
        <li>2.3.A Represent an algorithm that includes selection using written steps or a flowchart</li>
        <li>2.3.B Develop code using conditional statements, and determine the result of these statements</li>
        <li>2.3.C Develop code using nested conditional statements, and determine the result of these statements</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>2.4 Iteration
    <ul>
        <li>2.4.A Represent an algorithm that includes iteration using written steps or a flowchart</li>
        <li>2.4.B Develop code using iteration, and determine the result of iteration statements</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>2.5 Algorithm Development
    <ul>
        <li>2.5.A Develop algorithms by building new solutions or by reusing and combining existing solutions</li>
        <li>2.5.B Compare multiple algorithms to determine if they produce the same side effect or output</li>
    </ul>   
    </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>2.6 Errors
    <ul>
        <li>2.6.A Correct errors in programs</li>
        <li>2.6.B Develop code for test cases that can be used to check the correctness of an algorithm</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </li>

  </ul>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="unit2-summary-vocab">
    <title>Unit 2 Vocabulary</title>

  <p>Review the following Unit 2 vocabulary:</p>

     <activity label="vocab-review-2-1-design">
      <title>2-1 Design Vocabulary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 2-1. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 2-1 and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
        <premise>iterative development</premise>
        <response>developing a program through repeated cycles of design, implementation, and testing</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>incremental development</premise>
          <response>developing a program by in small steps adding new features or functionality</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>user research</premise>
          <response>the process of gathering information about users and their needs</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>program requirements</premise>
          <response>the specific needs and constraints that a program must meet to be successful</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>user stories</premise>
          <response>short, simple descriptions of a feature from the perspective of the user</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>storyboard</premise>
          <response>a visual representation of a sequence of events or scenes</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
      </activity>

    <activity label="vocab-review-2-1-copyright">
      <title>2-1 Copyright Vocabulary</title>
         <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 2-1. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 2-1 and try again.</p>
      </feedback>
      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise>copyright</premise>
          <response>a legal right that protects the creator's work from being used without permission</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>intellectual property</premise>
          <response>ownership for creations of the mind, like inventions, literary and artistic works</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>Creative Commons license</premise>
          <response>a type of license that allows creators to share their work with others while retaining some rights</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>open source software</premise>
          <response>software whose source code is available for anyone to view, modify, and distribute</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>Creative Commons Zero (CC0)</premise>
          <response>a license that waives all copyright and related rights in a work</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>

     <activity label="vocab-review-2-2-boolean">
      <title>2-2 Boolean Vocabulary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 2-2. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 2-2 and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
        <premise>Boolean</premise>
        <response>a data type that can only have the values True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>Boolean expression</premise>
          <response>an expression that evaluates to either True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>relational operator</premise>
          <response>an operator like <c>==</c> or <c>&lt;</c> that compares two values and 
            returns True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>logical operator</premise>
          <response>an operator like <c>and</c>, <c>or</c>, or <c>not</c> that combines Boolean 
            expressions and returns True or False</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>

     <activity label="vocab-review-2-3-selection">
      <title>2-3 Selection Vocabulary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 2-3. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 2-3 and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
        <premise>selection</premise>
        <response>the process of choosing which statements to execute based on certain conditions</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>conditional statement</premise>
        <response>a statement that checks a condition and executes different code depending on whether the condition is true or false</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>else</premise>
        <response>a keyword used in conditional statements to specify code that should be executed if the condition is false</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>nested conditionals</premise>
        <response>conditional statements that are placed inside other conditional statements</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>
    
  </subsection>
</section>